i-ethnic multicultural country it's  a journey that began under Tony Blair  with the appointment of people like  Valerie Amos Patricia Scotland Paul  botang and it continued with David  Cameron who brought on a generation of  these people who are now in the cabinet  and and indeed he asked me to do a  review so I welcome that labor remains  committed to these issues where the  natural party I think of diversity in  our country I think we've got a higher  percentage of MPS that come from an  ethnic background but I think all of us  can celebrate a multi-ethnic Britain  it's the Britain by the way that one is  the Olympics back in 2012. it's good to  see it mirrored in the conservative  party at this time  I'm glad you did mention David Cameron  and all to Tony Blair there because it  it was a lot of his moves to try and get  more um gender diverse uh more  ethnically diverse people into those  positions and that kind of helped get us  to where we are here but I don't want to  say this is not a partisansan issue at  all no exactly exactly not it's part of  the kind of country that we want to be  and outward facing inclusive country  that has reached all over the global  community and that's why by the way I  texted James cleverley and quasiquitang  yesterday we have good cordial  relationships and I congratulated them  on their appointment  and I'm sure they'll be very happy to  hear that for you miss Levy but I do  want to take you a little bit further on  this because then what responsibility to  having that diverse top team have for  those communities that they they  represent at the very top where we  haven't seen people like that before  well as I say I mean I do think we've  seen diversity  um in the cabinet but clearly  quasi-cratang's appointment and James  cleverly uh is important because these  are Big offices of State  all of us need Role Models whatever our  background uh and I and I hold that dear  particularly  for working people uh white and black  that need to see role models that look  like them are young people uh in public  life and so as I say I think we  celebrate this and I'm very pleased to  see big offices of State uh going to  these individuals and representing the  kind of country  um that we want to be recognizing that  there are many other countries within  the Democratic Western tradition that  have not arrived at that diverse place  yet  uh well just follow on from that then so  what does it tell us about Britain  particularly that we have managed to  arrive at this point as you put it  it reminds us that Britain uh is a  country with a history that reaches all  across the globe I think it reminds us  that we've now been on a journey really  since the Windrush generation came to  this country uh post the second world  war and and you start to see that second  third generation uh uh exceeding but I  think it also tells us that there's more  to do because that is not replicated  across every sector of our country we're  seeing it in politics in some ways  because of Acts of positive  discrimination by both the labor party  and indeed by David Cameron when he was  leader of the conservative party so I  think there's more to do but of course  we've got to welcome the diversity that  we're seeing in the current cabinet

